# Is Automation Worth the Time?

## Core Idea

Before automating a task, compare:

- The one-time time cost to design, write, test, and maintain the automation
- The repeated time cost of continuing to do the task manually

Automation is worthwhile when the time saved from repeated manual work becomes greater than the time spent creating the automation.

## Break-Even Formula

```text
time_to_automate < time_to_perform × number_of_times_done
```

Where:

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `time_to_automate` | Total one-time time needed to build, test, and deploy automation |
| `time_to_perform` | Time required to complete the task once manually |
| `number_of_times_done` | How many times the task will be repeated |

Make sure all time values use the same unit, such as minutes.

## Break-Even Point

The break-even number of repetitions is:

```text
break_even_runs = time_to_automate / time_to_perform
```

After that many runs, the automation begins saving time.

## Example

A task takes 40 minutes each week.

Creating the automation takes 10 hours.

Convert 10 hours to minutes:

```text
10 × 60 = 600 minutes
```

Set up the comparison:

```text
600 < 40 × weeks
```

Divide both sides by 40:

```text
15 < weeks
```

The automation breaks even after 15 weekly runs.

Beginning with the 16th week, the automated approach starts saving labor time.

## Quick Decision Table

| Situation | Likely decision |
|---|---|
| Task is rare and takes only a few minutes | Usually do it manually |
| Task is repeated often and takes meaningful time | Consider automation |
| Task has clear, predictable steps | Good automation candidate |
| Task needs human judgment each time | Keep human review involved |
| Automation could damage data or systems | Add testing, backups, and safeguards first |
| Automation saves time after a reasonable break-even point | Usually worthwhile |

## Time Is Not the Only Return

Automation can also create soft ROI (return on investment).

Soft ROI is harder to measure directly, but it can include:

- Better team collaboration
- Higher employee morale
- More employee motivation
- Greater job satisfaction
- Less boredom from repetitive work
- More time for problem-solving and higher-value tasks

## Decision Checklist

Before automating, ask:

- How long does the task take each time?
- How often does it happen?
- How long will it take to build and test the automation?
- How much maintenance will the automation need?
- When will the work break even?
- Does automation reduce errors or improve consistency?
- Are there soft benefits, such as freeing people for more meaningful work?
- Does the task require human approval or judgment?

## What to Remember

- Automation is an investment of time and effort.
- Convert units before comparing times.
- Find the break-even point to estimate when automation begins saving time.
- Repetitive, predictable tasks are the strongest candidates.
- Consider both measurable time savings and soft ROI.
- Include maintenance and safety costs when estimating the real value of automation.

